<TopicPage slug="subjects/history/foundational/medieval-world">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    The thousand years between Rome and the Renaissance were called "dark" by the people who came
    after — and the label stuck for centuries. It is mostly unfair. These centuries built the
    university, the castle, the cathedral, double-entry bookkeeping, and the idea that even kings
    answer to law. They also weathered the century's worst pandemic and produced the printing
    press, the technology that would end the medieval world from inside.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="a millennium in thirteen events — scrub from the fall of Rome to the reconnection of the hemispheres" widget="timeline" config={medievalTimeline} />

  <h2>Three things worth noticing</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>The "dark age" was brightest in Baghdad.</strong> The Abbasid House of Wisdom (founded c. 830) translated Greek science, invented algebra (al-Khwarizmi — his name gives us "algorithm"), and kept ancient learning alive while Europe rebuilt.</li>
    <li><strong>Law crept upward.</strong> Magna Carta (1215) began as a tax revolt and ended as a principle: the ruler is under the rules. Every later constitution inherits the move.</li>
    <li><strong>Catastrophe rearranged society.</strong> After the Black Death killed a third to half of Europe, labour became scarce and wages rose — serfdom weakened, not strengthened. Plague was an awful engine of change.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    Notice, too, how the timeline threads: printing (1455) makes arguments cheap, Constantinople's
    fall (1453) pushes Greek scholars west, and the oceans open (1492). The Renaissance and the
    modern world do not arrive — they assemble.
  </p>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Encyclopædia Britannica; Wickham, The Inheritance of Rome; standard chronologies of the Islamic Golden Age.</p>
